From 93e93a692a45e2d330328e64060ceed2cc5b69ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Boyd <danieljboyd@icloud.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:46:39 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] x11: hide, clip and destroy an OpenGL view's X window with
 the view

An NSOpenGLView's drawable is a child X window over the GNUstep window,
which the X server shows whatever the views around it draw.  XGXSubWindow
only moved it, so a hidden view, a view in a hidden ancestor, and the part
of a view scrolled out of a scroll view all stayed on screen.  -update now
unmaps the X window unless the view is visible, and clips it to the view's
visible rectangle with a bounding shape (the shape extension, which the x11
server already uses; without it the window is only unmapped).  It also
unmaps the window once the view has left the window it was made in.  The
window is created unmapped and mapped by -update.

Clearing the drawable left the X window on screen until the subwindow object
was deallocated, which an autorelease pool can put off; it is now destroyed
when the context detaches, once (the window's colormap is freed with it).
-setView: replaced the subwindow without destroying the old GLX window, which
was left on an X window destroyed later; the old drawable and window now go
first.  The GLX context is kept, so a view that attaches again draws with
the same context.

Closing a window destroys its X window, and the X server the GL window with
it; detaching then destroyed both again (BadWindow, GLXBadWindow).  The
subwindow now checks that the window it was made in still has that X window
before asking anything of its own, and the context attaches to the view
again when the window is shown again.

The placement code for -initWithView: and -update is shared, unchanged.

Tests/x11/openglSubwindow.m drives an NSOpenGLView's context directly
(-update, -clearDrawable, -setView:) and asks the X server: the child
window is unmapped while the view or an ancestor is hidden, when none of the
view is visible and after the view leaves its window; shaped to the visible
part of the view in a superview and in a scroll view, and GL drawing shows
only there; gone at once on -clearDrawable; only one left, with the same
GLX context, current, after attaching again; and no X error when the
window is closed, a new child window with the same context when it is shown
again.  Skips without GLX.  The test's sizes follow the window's scale
factor.

On Xvfb with Mesa llvmpipe: 29 of 29 pass, with or without the libs-gui
NSOpenGLView changes and with GSX11HandlesWindowDecorations NO; without
this change 13 fail.

